digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56729-6
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study investigates whether INO-3107, a DNA immunotherapy encoding HPV-6/HPV-11 antigens with an IL-12 adjuvant, can safely elicit HPV-specific immune responses in recurrent respiratory papillomatosis and provide clinical efficacy by reducing papilloma burden and reliance on repeated surgical removal.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When RRP is burdensome because of persistent HPV-6/11-driven papillomas and repeated surgery, and when HPV-specific immune enhancement is biologically and clinically supported, evaluating a targeted DNA immunotherapy is a coherent research aim."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) HPV-associated diseases are widespread and represent an important global public-health burden."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) A DNA immunotherapy approach such as INO-3107 is scientifically motivated for evaluation in RRP as a strategy to enhance HPV-specific immunity and potentially reduce disease burden."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Because repeated surgical removal of papillomas is the mainstay of RRP treatment and cumulative surgery increases laryngeal-injury risk, RRP management has a clinical rationale for approaches that reduce reliance on repeated surgery."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Prior DNA immunotherapies directed against HPV antigens, including IL-12-containing HPV DNA immunotherapy examples, have induced HPV-specific cellular immune responses and shown preliminary clinical activity in HPV-associated diseases including RRP."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Clinical studies of immunologic approaches in RRP, including PRGN-2012 and prophylactic HPV vaccination, support the pattern that enhancing HPV-specific immunity can reduce RRP burden or surgery frequency in some patients."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) INO-3107 is designed as a DNA immunotherapy that targets HPV-6/11 antigens and includes IL-12 adjuvant support to enhance cellular immune responses against those HPV antigens."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) RRP can be understood as an HPV-6/11-related respiratory tract disease whose lesion distribution can affect airway and voice functions."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because RRP papillomas show defective local anti-HPV cellular immunity, therapeutic vaccination is a plausible strategy to induce cellular immunity against established HPV-associated disease."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Insufficient local HPV-specific cytotoxic immunity is a plausible contributor to persistent HPV infection in RRP papillomas."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) RRP papillomas show localized immunosuppressive features, including TH2/Treg polarization and PD-1/PD-L1-related suppression, that are associated with defective HPV6/11 clearance and persistent HPV infection."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) HPV-associated diseases are widespread and constitute an emerging global epidemic, so HPV-related conditions represent an important global health burden."];
  "n15" [label="(6,0,0) Repeated surgical removal of papillomas for symptomatic management remains the mainstay of RRP treatment."];
  "n17" [label="(7,0,0) The risk of iatrogenic laryngeal injury increases with each additional surgery in RRP patients."];
  "n18" [label="(8,1,15) If the lifetime number of laryngeal surgeries is a significant risk factor for iatrogenic laryngeal injury, then higher surgical frequency causally increases injury risk."];
  "n2" [label="(1,1,5) If an infection such as HPV is the most common sexually transmitted infection worldwide, then diseases caused by that infection are likely to be highly prevalent and of major public health importance."];
  "n20" [label="(0,0,0) Conclusion: Given that the greater the number of lifetime surgeries the higher the risk of laryngeal injury and that RRP patients often require repeated surgeries for symptomatic relief, therefore the current surgery-based standard of care intrinsically increases cumulative risk of laryngeal injury and morbidity over time."];
  "n23" [label="(9,0,0) Patients with RRP exhibit suppressive or regulatory immune functions within papillomas rather than a cytotoxic response against HPV-infected cells, indicating a defective local anti-HPV immune response."];
  "n24" [label="(9,1,11) Therapeutic vaccination can induce cellular immunity against established HPV infections and lesions, offering a way to address HPV-associated disease in already-infected individuals."];
  "n25" [label="(13,1,3) RRP is characterized by polarized adaptive responses with TH2-like and Treg predominance and lacks TH1 and cytotoxic CD8+ T cells, supporting persistent HPV infection in papillomas."];
  "n27" [label="(10,1,29) Papilloma CD4+ T cells are enriched with adaptive regulatory T cells and show PD-1/PD-L1-related suppression, contributing to defective HPV6/11 clearance in RRP."];
  "n30" [label="(10,0,0) Previous DNA immunotherapies directed against HPV have induced potent HPV-specific immune responses, including cytotoxic T cells and CD8+ tumor infiltration, in adults with HPV-6-related RRP, HPV-related head and neck squamous cell carcinoma, and cervical disease."];
  "n35" [label="(9,1,26) DNA vaccines generally elicit cellular (including cytotoxic T cell) and humoral immune responses and provide long-lasting immunity, with practical advantages for repeated boosting and multi-antigen targeting."];
  "n38" [label="(11,0,0) Other groups have tested immunologic RRP treatments using antibody and T-cell-based approaches, and their clinical results support that modulating immunity can impact RRP disease course."];
  "n39" [label="(19,1,9) The adenoviral immunotherapy PRGN-2012 enhances HPV6/11-specific T cell immunity and shows a 50% complete response rate at the highest dose in severe RRP, with greater HPV-specific T-cell expansion in responders."];
  "n42" [label="(0,0,0) Conclusion: Given that multiple independent immunologic platforms (adenoviral T-cell therapy and prophylactic vaccines) can beneficially modify RRP by targeting HPV-specific immunity and that immune modulation is an effective disease-modifying strategy in RRP, therefore a DNA-based immunotherapy designed to enhance HPV-specific immune responses is a scientifically justified therapeutic strategy for RRP."];
  "n43" [label="(12,0,0) INO-3107 is a DNA immunotherapy that encodes antigens from both HPV-6 and HPV-11, directly targeting the etiologic HPV types in RRP."];
  "n44" [label="(12,0,0) INO-3107 also encodes interleukin-12 (IL-12) as an immune adjuvant to further enhance cellular immune responses against HPV-6/11 antigens."];
  "n5" [label="(2,0,0) HPV-6 and HPV-11 have a well-established etiologic role in RRP, implying that RRP is a prototypical disease directly driven by these low-risk HPV types."];
  "n9" [label="(3,0,0) RRP lesions can occur throughout the respiratory tract including oropharynx and larynx, so the disease can anatomically affect both voice and airway."];
